^(SLA SL1 export records material speed and profile identity values|SLA absolute correction erodes and expands every model slice|SLA area fill selects fast versus slow layer timing|SLA around-object and everywhere pads expose distinct public footprints|SLA around-object connector boundaries change pad geometry|SLA around-object pad gap boundaries change pad geometry|SLA around-object pads retain a footprint from real elevated supports|SLA attached SL1 archive rasterizes stepped model layers|SLA automatic support generation creates printable support geometry|SLA blocked model-facing supports anchor to the intervening body|SLA buildplate-only setting omits blocked support while retaining a clear buildplate route|SLA clearing a processed print releases object ownership|SLA cube processing creates one area-preserving model slice per layer|SLA drain holes transform with inverse-transpose normals and stick-out|SLA drain-hole edits invalidate the drill-hole step|SLA drain-hole normals remain perpendicular after rotated unequal scaling|SLA elephant foot correction fades by layer and preserves narrow features|SLA empty models remain empty through processing|SLA everywhere brim surrounds the model with a positive public footprint|SLA everywhere pads retain a positive product after filtering manual base points|SLA exposure durations determine fade-weighted layer statistics|SLA exposure limits accept inclusive endpoints and reject either side|SLA exposure validation rejects values outside printer bounds|SLA fast and slow tilt durations affect only their classified layers|SLA hollowing creates a smaller interior volume|SLA hollowing settings span registered endpoints in public geometry|SLA hollowing wall thickness contracts the public interior|SLA instance scale changes replace transformed print geometry|SLA large layers use the slow exposure statistics path|SLA layer height changes the number of sliced layers without changing volume|SLA layer-height changes invalidate slicing and alter layer count|SLA manual supports elevate the object and emit support slices|SLA material correction scales transformed slice geometry|SLA mesh getters expose only completed support and pad products|SLA model extension and deletion synchronize print objects by ID|SLA overlapping objects are unioned before material statistics|SLA pad brim rejects zero and expands through its valid range|SLA pad brim size expands the pad footprint|SLA pad geometry settings invalidate completed pads|SLA pad processing creates printable pad geometry and slices|SLA pad wall height honors its registered boundaries|SLA pad wall slope boundaries change the public footprint|SLA pad wall thickness controls pad height|SLA pad wall thickness honors its registered boundaries|SLA pad_around_object selects the standard or around-object pad|SLA pillar connection modes preserve intended near and far bridge topology|SLA raster-only changes invalidate merge while retaining object slices|SLA reapplying an unchanged model keeps completed state|SLA relative correction rebuilds transformed geometry|SLA secondary instance edits synchronize print instances|SLA slice-closing changes invalidate object slices|SLA statistics populate placeholders and output paths|SLA support geometry settings invalidate completed support trees|SLA support validation reports user-modified support points that are missing|SLA support-backed pads preserve both support and pad products|SLA supports_enable controls manual support production on one fixture|SLA tall isolated pillars acquire two printable neighbors|SLA task limits are reversible after finalize|SLA transformed support points follow the print transformation)$
